# Search filter

The publishing search-filter pattern narrows a result set with facets and adapts their placement on small screens. [Official publishing guidance](https://design-guide.publishing.service.gov.uk/patterns/search-filter/).

**Reference pattern.** The examples document a local adaptation; they do not add a publishing backend or a new Home Assistant capability.

## Share the filtering contract

The [search input](../../components/search-input.html) owns the query field, while this pattern owns how query, facets and results work together. The [finder](../frontend-templates/finder.html) composes this contract for a named collection. Its source-specific child pages do not define new filter algorithms or household services.

For these local examples, all query words must match a record, selected values within one Area facet are alternatives, and the query combines with the selected facet. Apply and Clear operate on the same fixed record set. Keep the visible count, result list and no-match message consistent. Without JavaScript, expose the records and keep submission disabled rather than sending the query to the current page. A real implementation needs its own permitted dataset, loading and error behaviour.

## When to use it

Use filters for a substantial device, automation or document catalogue. Choose facets that reflect the task, such as area, domain or availability. Keep a visible result count and clear selected criteria. A short list that is already easy to scan does not need an elaborate filter panel.

## Structure and behaviour

Group related choices with a fieldset and legend. Keep search, filters and results in a logical document order. A mobile disclosure may contain the same controls, with a link back to results. Native HA remains responsible for entity visibility and actual filtering; a documentation specimen is not a replacement entity registry.

## Home Assistant boundary

Automatic refresh must not move focus away from the changed filter. State when results are loading and when no records match. Preserve meaningful query state when navigating back. These local examples apply filters only when the person submits them: query words must all match a record, selected areas are alternatives, and query and area criteria combine. Clear filters restores the fixed dataset. Counts and empty-state messages reflect the actual visible records; no live entities are queried. Without JavaScript, all records remain readable and the form cannot submit.

